<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miao people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Miao are an ethnic group of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, and officially one of China's largest minorities. The name covers several related peoples, including the Hmong, who also live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. They rose repeatedly against Chinese rule, most notably in the Miao rebellions of the 18th and 19th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miao-people.jpg" alt="Miao people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quejaytee · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Long_Horn_Miao_in_Longga_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
